  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 345 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A wave of low pressure along a weakening frontal\nboundary brings scattered showers and storms to the area later \ntoday into tonight. Hot and very humid across NE NC before this\noccurs.\n\nThe latest WX analysis indicates WNW flow aloft, with a quasi-stationary\nfrontal boundary across northern portions of the CWA. For today,\nit generally appears that there will be limited precip coverage\nthis morning (at least for most of the region), then ramping up\nduring the aftn into the evening as the flow aloft becomes \nslightly more to the NW and eventually pushes the front south \nthrough the area later tonight. Dew pts are running in the mid- \nupper 70s across NE NC, and with the boundary expected to remain\nnorth through most of the day, decided to raise a Heat Advisory\nfrom Bertie Co. eastward to Perquimans, including Edenton and Elizabeth\nCity. It will only take highs into the low-mid 90s to lead to \nheat indices of 105+ for a few hrs with dew pts so high.\nElsewhere, may see heat indices approach 105 up to the VA-NC \nborder and southside Hampton Roads but confidence is too low for\nany headlines with more uncertainty as to the location of the \nsfc boundary and the potential for a wind shift to the NE. There\nis currently a more significant MCS well upstream from IL to \nwestern OH, and CAMs suggest that this partially holds together \nw/ scattered storms later this aftn and evening. A Marginal \nsevere risk is in place, as well as a Marginal ERO- both highly \ndependent on instability, which appears more likely for \nsouthern 1/2 of the area and more uncertain across the far north\nand ern shore. Locally heavy rainfall will be possible given \nhighs PWATs but storms should be moving, so a widespread heavy \nrain event is currently not expected if convection develops \ntomorrow afternoon/evening (but with localized flooding \npossible, mainly in urban or flood- prone areas).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonable and Saturday with isolated showers W\nof the Bay, a higher chance for afternoon showers/storms Sunday.\n\nSaturday looks mostly dry and fairly pleasant across the NE 1/2\nof the area as sfc high pressure to our N ridges closer to the\narea. Farther W/SW, expect isolated to widely scattered showers\nthough thunder chances are fairly low. WPC has a MArginal ERO\nacross portions of south central VA and interior NE NC though\nthe heavier rain will most likely be W/SW of the CWA. Highs \nmostly in the mid to upper 80s/around 90F in the wake of the \ncold front. The upper ridge starts to build back into the \nregion Saturday night/Sunday, and some of the models even depict\nshowers Saturday night. Scattered showers and thunderstorms \nwill return to the forecast on Sunday, along with highs back \naround 90F for most of the area, and PoPs are now a little\nhigher with a Marginal ERO across most of the area and a Slight\nbrushing our far NE. The expansive upper ridge across the \nsouthern U.S. will finally start to break down and flow aloft \nwill become more zonal. The current forecast currently has the \nnormal diurnally driven afternoon/evening isolated to scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Hot and humid with afternoon storms Monday \nahead of the next cold front.\n\nMonday still looks hot with afternoon storms possible. Heat \nAdvisories may be needed across at least the SE half of the \nforecast area with heat index 105-100 in the forecast even after\nsome manual adjustments accounting for the high dew pt bias of\nthe NBM. More seasonable Tue-Wed.",
